    "completenessNote": "逐条对译，共 1 条；条号与官方原文一一对应，未合并、未省略。",
    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "Supreme People's Court\nNotice on Repealing Some Judicial Interpretations\nIssued before the End of 1993\n27 July 1994      Fa Fa No. 16 [1994]\nThe higher people's courts of all provinces, autonomous regions and municipalities directly under the Central Government, and the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army:\nIn accordance with the requirement put forward by President Ren Jianxin in the report to the Sixteenth National Conference of Courts that the judicial interpretations of the past be promptly reviewed and that those which do not meet the requirements of accelerating reform and opening up and economic development be revised, supplemented or repealed as the case may be, our Court has, since 1993 and on the principle of proceeding from the near to the distant, in stages and in batches, and with all dispatch, carried out a comprehensive review of the judicial interpretations issued between 1979 and 1993. The list of the first batch of judicial interpretations whose repeal has been adopted upon discussion by the Judicial Committee of our Court is hereby issued to you. A total of 11 judicial interpretations are repealed in this batch, including 2 relating to criminal trial, 2 relating to economic trial, 4 relating to administrative trial and 3 relating to maritime trial. These repealed judicial interpretations shall no longer apply from the date of issuance of this Notice (some of them had long ceased to be effective of themselves). However, judgments and rulings rendered in the relevant cases by applying the above judicial interpretations remain valid. The review of judicial interpretations is still under way, and judicial interpretations that need to be repealed will be notified to you in batches in the future. Where a document of the nature of a judicial interpretation jointly issued by the Supreme People's Court and a relevant department needs to be repealed, our Court will jointly issue a document with that department to repeal it. Where only part of the content of some judicial interpretations no longer meets the needs of current trial practice, our Court will, on the basis of a comprehensive review, organise the relevant departments to study and revise it; all localities are requested to report their opinions to our Court in a timely manner.\nAttachment: List of Judicial Interpretations Issued before the End of 1993 Repealed by the Supreme People's Court (First Batch)\nAttachment:\nSupreme People's Court\nList of Judicial Interpretations Issued before the End of 1993\nRepealed (First Batch)\n(Discussed and adopted at the 667th meeting of the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court)\nNo.\nCategory\nTitle of judicial interpretation\nDate of promulgation and document number\nReason for repeal\n1\nCriminal\nOpinions of the Supreme People's Court on How to Apply the Law in Handling Cases of Joint Theft\n12 April 1991, Fa (Yan) Fa No. 11 [1991]\nSuperseded by the 'Interpretation on Several Questions concerning the Specific Application of Law in Handling Theft Cases' issued by the Supreme People's Court and the Supreme People's Procuratorate on 11 December 1992.\n2\nNotice of the Supreme People's Court on Severely Combating the Criminal Activity of Crossing the Border Illegally\n24 September 1993, Fa Fa No. 24 [1993]\nOn 5 March 1994 the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress adopted and promulgated the 'Supplementary Provisions on Severely Punishing the Crime of Organising or Transporting Other Persons to Cross the National Border (Frontier) Illegally'; the above judicial interpretation made on the basis of the relevant provisions of the Criminal Law is no longer applicable.\n3\nEconomic\nReply of the Supreme People's Court on Jurisdiction over Suits Arising from Disputes concerning Oral Agreements\n16 March 1990\nOn 9 April 1991 the National People's Congress adopted and promulgated the 'Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China'; the relevant content of the above Reply is inconsistent with that Law or duplicates it, and is no longer applicable.\n4\nReply of the Supreme People's Court on Whether a People's Court Shall Accept a Suit Brought by a Party after an Administrative Department for Industry and Commerce Has Handled a Property Dispute Arising from an Invalid Economic Contract\n3 November 1990, Fa (Jing) Fu No. 16 [1990]\nSuperseded by the 'Reply Letter on the Acceptance of Cases concerning Dissatisfaction with the Decision of an Administrative Department for Industry and Commerce on the Confirmation of the Invalidity of an Economic Contract and on the Handling of Property Loss, Which Shall Be Regarded as Administrative Cases' issued by the Supreme People's Court on 1 April 1992.\n5\nAdministrative\nInterim Provisions of the People's Court on Several Questions concerning the Specific Application of Law in the Trial of Public Security Administrative Cases\n24 October 1986, Fa (Yan) Fa No. 31 [1986]\nOn 4 April 1989 the National People's Congress adopted and promulgated the 'Administrative Litigation Law of the People's Republic of China'; the above judicial interpretation is inconsistent with that Law and is no longer applicable.\n6\nReply of the Supreme People's Court on How to Apply Article 13 of the Land Administration Law and Article 14 of the Forest Law\n31 July 1987, Fa (Jing) Fu Zi No. 28 [1987]\nOn 4 April 1989 the National People's Congress adopted and promulgated the 'Administrative Litigation Law of the People's Republic of China'; the above Reply is inconsistent with that Law and is no longer applicable.\n7\nReply of the Supreme People's Court on Whether a Railway Transport Court Shall Accept Public Security Administrative Cases\n11 September 1987, Fa (Yan) Fu No. 34 [1987]\nOn 4 April 1989 the National People's Congress adopted and promulgated the 'Administrative Litigation Law of the People's Republic of China'; the above Reply is inconsistent with that Law and is no longer applicable.\n8\nReply of the Supreme People's Court on Whether the 'Interim Provisions of the People's Court on Several Questions concerning the Specific Application of Law in the Trial of Public Security Administrative Cases' Apply to the Trial of Other Administrative Cases\n13 January 1988, Fa (Yan) Fu No. 9 [1988]\nOn 4 April 1989 the National People's Congress adopted and promulgated the 'Administrative Litigation Law of the People's Republic of China'; the above Reply is inconsistent with that Law and is no longer applicable.\n9\nMaritime\nReply of the Supreme People's Court on the Procedure for the Arrest of Ships before the Issuance of the Provisions on the Arrest of Ships\n24 October 1981, Fa (Jiao) Zi No. 3 [81]\nSuperseded by the 'Provisions on the Arrest of Ships before Litigation by Maritime Courts' issued by the Supreme People's Court on 6 July 1994.\n10\nSpecific Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on the Arrest of Ships before Litigation (Adopted by the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court on 31 January 1986)\nSuperseded by the 'Provisions on the Arrest of Ships before Litigation by Maritime Courts' issued by the Supreme People's Court on 6 July 1994.\n11\nSpecific Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on the Compulsory Sale of Arrested Ships in Discharge of Obligations\n29 August 1987, Fa (Jing) Fa No. 22 [1987]\nSuperseded by the 'Provisions on the Auction of Arrested Ships in Discharge of Obligations by Maritime Courts' issued by the Supreme People's Court on 6 July 1994."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1."
        }
      }
    ]
